\pard\plain \fi720\sl288\slmult1\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 By Tim Burkhead}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Cueva del Tajo de las Figuras is hardly a cave by archaeological standards, more of a scoop in a vertical sandstone cliff, but inside, it feels as though im within the enfolding walls of a womb.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Multi-talented, Willoughby Verner was a soldier, military historian, natural historian, artist, and intrepid adventurer obsessed by virds. Photographs during his heyday in the 1880s show him with a luxuriant moustache, broad brimmed hat and an abundance of leather straps and belts and loops bearing his hunting paraphernalia, camera, and field glasses.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 At the time, rural spain was a challenging and depressing place for travellers. Richard Ford, in his 1845 Handbook for Travleers in Spain, warned of cheap inns whos bedroom walls \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91are often stained with the marks of nocturnal combat\u8217\'92\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 of bedbugs, or as he calls them, \u8216\'91french ladybirds\u8217\'92. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Despite his gammy leg.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 It seems clear\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 inasmuch as anything in cave art is clear \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  that the artist cared only about the conformation of the birds\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92 head and body, for it is these features that shout \u8216\'91bustard\u8217\'92. The legs on the other hand, are clunky, stumpy and perfunctory, drawn with two ochre-dipped fingers in a single stroke.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8230\'85proposed that cave images were created by shamans whose objective was to use self-induced hallucinations to deal with supernatural phenomena, such as drought (by summoning rain) or sickness (by seeking cures). This hypothesis was devised mainly to explain teh otehrwise unintelligbile geometric shapes that decorate many caves, including Pileta and, to a lesser extent, El Tajo. Such patterns, it was argued were visiual representations of \u8216\'91entopic\u8217\'92 phenomena\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  that is, visual effects from within the eye itself\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  the kinds of things you see during a migraine and \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  I am told \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  during altered states of consciousness as a result of taking hallucinogenic drugs. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Four million mummified birds\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 mainly sacred Ibises \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  discovered in the vast subteranean catacombs at Tuna el-Gebel in Ebypt in the early 1800s. Encased in ceramic jars or wooden coffins, the mummies were stacked meteres high in subterranean cells carved from the soft desert rock.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 It was also the shift from nomadism to more settled living, for the cultivation of crops such as wheat forced farmers to live near their fields. Dependency on one or two agricultural plants made farmers vulnerable to the vagaries of the weather \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  floods and droughts \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  and it was probably this that bound the agricultural Revolution to a religious revolution: devotion to gods in exchange for abundant harvests, and sets of shared beliefs that allowed people to work together.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 In tutankhamun\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s tomb, we see the boy-king as an archer hunting ostriches with dogs from a chariot. There are birds caught by cats; birds  mobbing a mongoose attempting to steal tehri chicks; a hovering pied Kingfisher about to plunge into the water. In others, tehre are men with throwing sticks; capturing flocks of ducks in clap-nets, and netting golden orioles to protect teh sycomore figs on which they feed\u8230\'85men force-feeding geese.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The sheer numbers of ibis mummies show that the Sacred Ibis was once abundant in Egypt. But no more. The species now is extinct there as a breeding bird, one of the world\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s many birds in decline as a result of human-induced habitat change.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Aristotle\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8230\'85His suggestion is that a trade-off exists between teh size of certain parts of the body and copulation ferquency. The thinness or weakness of certain birds\u8217\'92 legs, he tells us, makes them prone to copulation, adding that \u8216\'91this applies also to human beings\u8217\'92. As he explains, this is because the nourishment that was intended for the legs is diverted into semen. Because of their short, thick legs, raptors \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  he says \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  do not copulate very frequently.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Although not all copulations ended in the necessary \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91cloacal kiss\u8217\'92 that signalled successful insemination, there was still an average of fifty-nine inseminatinos per clutch. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Aristotle\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s approach was one that characterized science for much of its history. All areas of study go through an observation phase \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  equivalent to Aristotle\u8217\'92s amassing of information. Then follows a period of trying to make sense of those observations; attempting to find some generalities or patterns that provide general explanations. Aristotle\u8217\'92s followers in the next 1,500 years, however, saw his explanations as final. It was as if he had opened the door to understanding, then pulled it firmly shut behind him, because nothing more was required.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The realization that birds acquire their song from a paren twas probably derived from aristotle\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s observation that if young birds \u8216\'91have been removed from teh nest and have heard other birds singing\u8230\'85some sing a different note from teh parent birds.\u8217\'92 I can appreciate why this made such an impression on Aristotle. I once had a pet Eurasian Siskin that had been reared by canary foster parents, and instead of uttering the typical wheezy siskin refrain, this little bird belted out pure canary song, an incongruity that stopped me in my tracks every time I heard it.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 As a pair, they anticipate a situation that persists today: the careful, intellectually innovative scientific type \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 Aristotle\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 contrasted with the enthusiastic, sometimes careless popularizer, Pliny.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Pliny started his vast natural history encyclopedia around AD 70, when his friend Vespasian was emperior, and completed it some seven years later. The book, among the largest from ancient Rome, and the only one of Pliny\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s to have survived, spanned mineralogy, geology, astronomy, botany and zoology, with information gleaned from a huge array of sources, including Aristotle.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 For 1,500 years, Pliny\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s works dominated all thoughts about the natural world. It was only once the scientific revolution began its reassessment of ancient knowledge in the 1600s that people began to question Pliny\u8217\'92s authority. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Eagles often lay more eggs than they rear, and usually one chick is killed by its older sibling, especially if food is short. This \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91brood reduction\u8217\'92, as it is known, has evolved because\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 strange as it might seem\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  it results in the eagles leaving more descnendants overall throughout their lifetime.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 To his credit, Pliny says that the idea that peacocks are both vain and spiteful \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91in the just the same way that a goose is \u8220\'93bashful\u8221\'94, appears to me to be utterly unfounded\u8217\'92. Similarly, he debunks the idea that \u8216\'91at the moment of a swan\u8217\'92s death, it gives utterance to a mournful song\u8217\'92\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 swan song. He says: \u8216\'91This is an error, in my opinion, at least I have tested the truth of the story on several occasions.\u8217\'92}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Romans ate thrushes, presumably winter migrants, captured and then fattened in special aviaries. Some at least were allowed to fly out from a roasted wild boar as it was cut open at the table\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 a forerunner fof the four and twenty blackbirds baked in a pie. The tongues of birds were popular among roman epicures and no more so than those of the nigthingale. It seems unlikely that these really were tongues that were eaten. A nightingale\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s tongue, like that of most small birds, is barely worth the effort, consisting of little more than two barely digestible hyoid bones and a few meagre scraps of muscle. \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 what were commonly referred to as larks\u8217\'92 \u8216\'91tongues\u8217\'92 were actually their breast muscles, which were much more substantial and tasty. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The first-century cookbook by Apicius, whom Pliny the Elder described as \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91the most insatiable gorger of all gluttons\u8217\'92, includes a recipe for flamingo and is responsible for establishing the idea that the flamingo\u8217\'92s tongue was the ultimate in Roman gourmandizing. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Academic interested in medieval birds\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8230\'85He spread his net wide and, as well as scrutinizing the birds in the Bayeux Tapestry, he analysed, identified adn reported on birds in misericords, missals, psalters and bibles as well as in the great treatise on falconry written I nthe early thirteenth century by the holy roman emporer Frederick II..}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The bird marginalia.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Nick fox - 1995}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93When a falconer flies his falcon, part of his heart flies with her. This is the difference between the falconer and a man with a gun. The wise falconer can feel the spirit of a falcon, like an aura.. The spirit of a horse or dog is different because they have a desire to please whereas the spirit of the falcon is independent, self reliant and usually stronger than man\u8217\'92s. Daily contact with such spirit enhances, inspires and strengthens our own.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 And I saw an angel standing in the sun; and he cried with a loud voice, saying to all the fowls that fly in the midst of heaven, come and gather yourselves together\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8230\'85that ye may eat the flesh of kings, and\u8230\'85of horses, and\u8230\'85of all men.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Frederick II - was ostensibly the child of Henry VI and COnstance, queen regnant of Sicily, but there was some doubt about his true parentage. Constance was forty when he was born and it was rumored that she had faked her pregancy, with the new-born Frederick smuggled into the royal palace. Like his father, Frederick duly became king of Germany, Holy Roman Emperor and king of Sicily. He proved exceptional in many ways and was dubbed the stupor mundi\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  the astonishment of the world. Frederick was unconstrained by any religious beliefs and Pope Gregory IX considered him to be the predecessor of the Antichrist, so he excommunicated him. Intellectually curious, perceptive and unorthodox, Frederick was a keen falconer, but he was unique in his day in recognizing that, to understand birds of prey, he had to understand birds in general. His extraordianry manuscript covers the anatomy, ecology and migration of birds, fully justifying the later claim that he was the father of ornithology. \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  The Art of Hunting with Birds.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Because falconry was expensive, time-consuming and, above all, useless\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  the ultimate signal of consipcuous consumption\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  it is not surprising that it eventually turned round and lunged at its practitioners. Mediveal nobles in Europe, like much of the prey they pursued with their Gyrs and Peregrines, were soft targets.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The lower classes, excluded from participating, despised the falcon-fisted nobility for their right to ride their horses and dogs through their crops in pursuit of theri hunting birds.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Edmund Bert\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s Treatise of Hawks and Hawking of 1619\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91In teaching a hawk it was useless to bludgeon the creature into submission.. The old hawk masters had invented a means of taming them which offered no visible cruelty, and whose secret cruelty had to be borne by the trainer as well as by the bird. They kept the bird awake.. For a space of two, three, or as much as nine nights.. All the time he treated his captive with more than every courtesy, more than every kindness and consideration.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 After several mutually sleepless nights White enters his mews, where the goshawk \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91regarded me with tolerant contempt. He had no doubts about who was the slave, the ridiculous and subservient one who stood and waited.\u8217\'92 When the bird finally deigns to jump on White\u8217\'92s hand and eat, it is \u8216\'91an exultatation! What a bursting heart of gratitude and triumph..the rest of the day was a glow of pleasure.\u8217\'92}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Dix Harwood in his book, Love for Animals and How It Developed in Great Britain, \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91 the evidence fo the sympathetic interest in animals before 1700 is very slight.\u8217\'92}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 He distinguished betwen the grop (a storage structre) and the gizzard (the true stomach)}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The fact that the nahua people had specific terms for differnet patches of quetzal plumage on the birds body signifies\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  like the many Yupik and Inuit words for snow\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  just how improtant these were for them. No one killed quetzals; the birds were trapped using birdlime and then released so that their regrown plumes coudl be taken another day.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Featherworks have been found in elite ancient Peruvian burial sites dating back to 3000-2000 bc, and , as is clear from wall paintings found at the archaeological sites of teotihuacan adn bonampak, mexiacn and mayan nobles, respectively, had been using feathers in headresses to signify their status for centuries.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 story behind each feather on a headress.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 This arrogation of local knowledge by colonial powers has been criticized for \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91purposely effacing the lives of subaltern actors\u8217\'92..}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Subaltern-under other}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Puffins - As female birds age, their ovaries and oviducts in every one of these dark-faced puffins were in a bad way. Non-functional ovaries are unable to produce oesterogen, and in birds such as pheasants and peafowl it is nown that the lack of this hormone causes females to change sex and moult into male plumage\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 much to the bewilderment and consternation of their owners.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Dove-grey and doe-eyed, the fulmar petrel - the northern fulmar -  is a beautiful mini-albatross. But looks can be deceptive, and this is a bird with a sharp bill a rank smell and the unsavoury habit of projecting a stream of hot, oily vomit into the face of the unwary. It was the uncanny accuracy of the fulmar\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s projectile vomiting that gave rise to its name: foul mar (foul gull).}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Like most other seabirds, fulmars lay a single large egg, averaging around 100g, so rougly twice as heavy as a typical chicken egg, but also relatively large for the size of the bird. Not only that, a fulmar egg contains a relatively large youlk, so each one is a nutritious prize. Acquiring them from the grassy slopes and rocky ledges of the gigantic cliffs demands the same technique that was used to collect eggs from teh guillemot breeding ledges. Teams of ten or more men carry huge hemp ropes to the cliff tops, where they hammer in two wooden wstakes to secure one end of the rope. Then, wearing a robust sheepskin harness attached to the rope, and handcrafted sheeps wool non-slip \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91slippers\u8217\'92, one man is lowered down to the ledges where the fulmars nest. Once among the birds the man steps out of the harness so that he has more freedom to move around \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  a foolhardy and dangerous game of machismo that mroe than once has resulted ina fatal fall. In 1857, no fewer than eight men died during the egg harvest. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The fulmar chick is a slowcoach, remaining some six weeks in the nest, during which time it is fed a concentrated oily mix of marine offal and plankton by its parents before fledging. The result: one fat chick, weighing more than each of its paretns, with almost half its bodyweight fat \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  a food reserve \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  a tactic that has evolved to see the young bird through its first few weeks of independent life. But, of course, it is that fat that makes the fulmar chick such a tasty dish.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The current harvest of recently fledged fulmars in the faroes is huge and harsh. At fledging the young fulmar is so fat that does little more than flop down onto the sea from its nest site onthe cliffs, where it bobs around like a blob of lard floating in a washing up bowl. Using high powered boats and a long-handled net, the locals scoop them up in their hundreds or thousands. I was told of one boat that collected 10,00 chicks in a single season. There are no legal limits and some 40-80,000 fulmar chicks are sold each year in the islands\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92 capital, plus those retained for home conspumption. The birds are ruthlessly processed as they are brough aboard: heads ripped off and the oesophagus and gizzard dragged out (to avoid their oily contents) and discarded. Plucked and singed to remove the down with a blow torch( which adds a certainly smokiness to the taste), guted and eventually roasted in someone\u8217\'92s oven, the fatty young fulmar is served and eaten with potatoes.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 For Martin and his colleagues the consequences of this overegged diet was, he noted, diabolical. Some of the men had \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91the hemorrhoid veins swelled\u8217\'92, and Martin tells us that \u8216\'91Mr Campbel adn I were at no small trouble before we coudl reduce them to their ordinary temper\u8217\'92. They treated themselves with a \u8216\'91glister\u8217\'92 (enema) made from teh \u8216\'91teh roots of sedges, fresh butter\u8217\'92 and salt, which, being administered, had its wished for effect. The inhaibats, martin says, \u8216\'91reckoned this an extraordinary performance, being it seems, the first of this kind they had ever occasion to hear of\u8217\'92. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Among Darwin\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s non-scientific supporters, one of the most influential was the writer the Reverend Charles Kingsley, later author of The Wwater-Babies. Knowing he was sympathetic towards science, Darwin sent kingsley a pre-publication copy of the Origin. In response, kingsley told him that the origin awed him: \u8216\'91if you be right I must give up much that I have believed\u8217\'92. For Kingsley, natural selection was no \u8216\'91obstacle to faith: God didn\u8217\'92t just make the world, he made the world make itself\u8217\'92.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 For a long time I thought this was attributed to Teilhard de Chardin}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 At the time Origin appeared, Gould was busy producing his multi-volume series on hummingbirds. These tiny birds were Gould\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s ruling passion. Like Allan Hume and other wealthy, obsessive bird collectors, Gould sent his own men out in search of rarer and rarer hummingbird specimens, tempting them with substantial financial rewards, including 50$ (equavalient to about $4,000 today) for a Marvellous Spatuletail\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  which he failed to secure. Over just a few years, Gould amassed thousands of skins, nests and eggs of some 320 of the 350 known hummingbird species, and his exhibition of these in purpose-built glass cabinets in a specially constructed building at the zoological gardens as part of the Great Exhibiition of 1851 was a spectacle few had ever seen the like of before. The tens of thousands of visitors were entranced and, inevitably perhaps, Gould\u8217\'92s show triggered a dmaaging fashion for hummingbird displays and, subsequently, as accoutrements for ladies\u8217\'92 hats and dresses: a tsunami of hummingbird slaughter. Not that Gould cared: the Great Exhibition was the best publicity possible for his five-volume Monograph of the Trochilidae, or Family of Hummingbirds, then in progress.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 German bird-catcher and writer Johann Conrad Aitinger suggested using ash, alum and sulphur to help preserve the skin, which was then pulled over a straw \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91corpus\u8217\'92, using wires and skewers to hold the bird in a lifelike posture. These specimens still needed to be dried over a fire \u8216\'91every quarter of a year\u8217\'92  to protect them from the scourge of moth and beetle larvae that continue to be the occasional bane of museum curators to this day. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Arsenic soap smeared on the inner surfacce of a bird specimen meant that study skins were no longer vulnerable to the jaws of voracious insect larvae. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 How did people like allan hume, Waltaer Rothschild, and Emilie Snethlage justify their level of killing?}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Hunting and collecting have always been obsessions among the wealthy, whether it was the pharoahs fowling in the marshes and filling their tombs with artefacts, inca cchiefs with their menageries, or Europeans like Ole Worm and Francis Willughby cramming their cabinets with curiositeis. The obsession with bird collecting in the 1800s and 1900s was a continuation of that, but much more widespread, because by this date a higher proportion of people in Europe had the wealth and time to collect. Acquisition adn accumulation were\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 and still are\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 deep-rooted cravings for status.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The worlds\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92 museums currently contain around ten million bird skins. Over 99 per cent of the 10-11,000 known bird species are represented in these collections. No other animal grou, except mammals, of which there are far fewer species, is so well represented in museum collections, and as such they represent the most remarkable scientific resource.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Ten Million birds in museums? I can feel you squirming uncomfortably, but wait. Domestic cats kill this number of birds Every Day, adn similar numbers are destroyed by hunters, habitat loss, and climate change. And with respect to chickens\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8230\'85each day sees the death of over 100 million individuals.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The attitudes of American adn European ornithologists towaards collecting differ dramatically\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 a difference that may reflect national characters: American enthusiasm verses European reserve; guns versus no guns. I asked a seniro ornithologist closely and impartially involved in the collecting debate how the difference arose and why it persists. Wealth, or more politely, funds, he suggested, are at teh heart of it. For europeans, the cost of two world wars together with the loss of imperial territories pretty well ptu an end to overseas collecting, and Wlater Rothschild\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s fall from grace was almost the final nail int eh European collecting coffin.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 American museums continue to be supported by wealthy benefactors, allowing them to maintain their collecting tradition. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Killing adult birds is potentially more damaging to a population than taking eggs. This is because many birds have evolved to cope with high levels of nest predation and will often re-lay right away if a clutch is taken. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The battle between collectors and the authorities is epitomized by the stealing of Scotland\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s Loch Garten osprey eggs in 1958. Having become extinct in Britain in 1916, one pair of Western Ospreys returned to Scotland to breed in 1954 and drew egg collectors like sharks to blood. The eggs were stolen. Increased security foiled subsequent attempts, and then, in an act of frustrated revenge, egg collectors felled the nest tree in 1986.}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 For collectors like these, their boyhood hobby had metamorphosed into an adult obsession. Psychologists exploring the motivation behind extreme collecting have identified a number of traits that predispose men to become pathological collectors. One of these is insecurity, usually through the loss of a parent as a child, and the subsequent need to compensate as an adult by accumulating \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91stuff\u8217\'92. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Cocker, for example, likened bird lvoers to a tribe among which an amateur anthropologist such as himself could identify eight separate sub-clans: 1.) scientist 2.) ornithologist, 3.) bird-watcher 4.)birdwatcher 5.) birder 6.) twitcher 7.) dude 8.) robin stroker. Of these, he says, scientist and ornithologist overlap almost completely; the next four categories have much in common too, although Cocker is at pains to distinguish the bird-watcher\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 which he identifies as being like the train-spotter\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  from the birdwatcher, preferring unhyphenated authenticity, or better still, birder, the term now in most common use. A twitcher, he says, is someone who, as we will see, pursues rarities, and is often considered \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91the most evil creature alive..fanatical, self-indulgent, uncaring, comptetitive and anti-environmentl\u8217\'92. Dudes are posers, pretending to know more about birds than tehy do and universally despised by those above them in the avian hierarchy. The final sub-clan is what Cocker callse the \u8216\'91robin strokers\u8217\'92 \hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  those people who watch birds from their living-room window. These, he says\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  inc case anyone thinks his term disaparaging\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0  comprise \u8216\'91the vast bulk of decent folk without whom bird conservation would have no real teeth.\u8217\'92}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Edward O. Wilson\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s \u8220\'93Sociobiology\u8221\'94-expert on social insects\hich\f0 \emdash \loch\f0 left wing biologists saw Wilson\u8217\'92s book as justifying right-wing political policies, something that Wilson vehementlyh denied, sparking a controversy that was put to rest only in 2000 with the publication of Finnish sociologist Ullica Segerstrale\u8217\'92s blockbuster Defenders fo the Truth.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Brood parasitism - cuckoo}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 In 1785, the adventurer George Cartwright, appalled by the relentless commercial slaughter of Great Auks on Funk Island, off the northeastern Newfoundland coast, commented that unless the killing ceased, \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91the whole breed will be diminished to almost nothing\u8217\'92. This is exxactly what happened because as the bird became increasingly scarce, the market price for eggs and skins soared\u8230\'85and the killing continued.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Sentimentality towards animals was deemed both \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91unscientific\u8217\'92 and unmanly, a controversy that Darwin himself was caught up in. A self-avowed animal lover, darwin abhorred cruelty, but also acknowledged that some experiments on live animals, usually dogs, were necessary to further our understanding of physiology and medicene.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 Not everyone approved of the new law: \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91a fowler\u8217\'92 wrote to the Yorkshire Gazette asking what use or ornament seabirds were to people, if they weren\u8217\'92t there to be shot at? Bu the act had teeth, for on 10 July 1869 Barnes-Lawrence noted with satisfaction in his diary how a Mr Tasker of Sheffield, who openly shot twenty-eight birds in defiance of the law, was apprehended and fined 2/6 for each bird and 9/- costs: a total of $3,19s. }
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 What got the Seabird Protection Act through Parliament was that it was less about preserving seabirds than about protecting people\loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8217\'92s lives and livelihoods. \u8216\'91The cries of the seabirds at their breeding colonies, it was argued, warned fog-bound mariners of the proximity of rocky shores, aggregations of sesabirds at sea showed fishermen where to cast tehir nets and gulls following the plough helped farmers by devouring innumerable noxious invertebrates.}
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1 The wings, tails and plumes of birds such as kittiwakes, and more exotic species like egrets, birds of paradise, tanagers and hummingbirds, incorporated into hats became synonymous with edwardian fashion. }
\par\plain \f0\fs24\b0\i0\cf1
